{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 Calibri;}}\f0\fs24 Seed enquiry checklist: specify retail pack sizes and labelling language \u183? Questions and answers\par A seed enquiry is most useful when it separates what you want to buy from how you want it packed, labelled and delivered. This guide answers common questions about preparing a clear reseller enquiry and reading the supplier's reply, based on the checklist principle that product records, packaging records and delivery records should stay distinct. It does not quote prices or promise availability; those are confirmed only in the quotation.\par \b What is the purpose of a structured seed enquiry?\b0\par A structured enquiry gives the supplier a clear basis for checking what can be supplied. Its aim is not to place an order but to describe requirements precisely enough that the supplier can respond with a quotation. Three records should be kept apart: the products and catalogue quantities being requested, the retail packaging and labelling requirements, and the delivery details. Mixing them up makes it hard for a supplier to tell which part is a product request and which is a packaging preference.\par \b Which details should I record for every item?\b0\par For each seed item, record the botanical name, variety and catalogue reference. Add the catalogue quantity and the unit used in the catalogue request, because the unit also helps distinguish similarly named varieties. Then record the intended retail pack size and the unit that defines it, and the required labelling language. Finally note the destination country and postal code, the requested delivery date, and your business contact details. Where practical, repeat the pack size and labelling language on each product line so neither requirement becomes detached from the correct variety.\par \b Why separate catalogue quantities from retail pack sizes?\b0\par A catalogue quantity and unit do not by themselves confirm the pack size or labelling language needed for resale. If you treat the catalogue unit as the retailer's packaging requirement, the supplier may review the wrong specification. A useful enquiry therefore answers three practical questions separately: which variety, reference, quantity and catalogue unit are requested; which retail pack size and labelling language should be reviewed; and where and by which date the shipment should go. Available pack sizes are confirmed in the quotation, so present pack size and labelling language as requested requirements, not as agreed arrangements.\par \b Is there a copy-ready format I can follow?\b0\par Yes. For each product, write a line such as: Product \u8212? botanical name, variety, catalogue reference. Catalogue request \u8212? quantity and catalogue unit. Retail packaging \u8212? requested pack size and pack-size unit. Labelling \u8212? required labelling language. Destination \u8212? country and postal code. Requested delivery date \u8212? date. Business contact \u8212? name and contact details. Repeat the product, packaging and labelling lines for each item. If several products share one pack specification, still show every catalogue reference so the link between products and that specification stays clear.\par \b What should I check before sending?\b0\par Run a short final check. Does every item have a botanical name, variety and catalogue reference? Does every requested quantity carry a unit? Are the retail pack size and its unit recorded separately from the catalogue request? Is the labelling language stated for each relevant item, rather than left as a general note? Are the destination country, postal code and requested delivery date included? Are your business contact details present? And are pack sizes and labelling arrangements presented as requests rather than confirmed terms? A quick test: can a reader tell which product is involved, how much is requested, what pack and labelling language should be reviewed, and where and when delivery is intended?\par \b What should I look for in the supplier's quotation?\b0\par Submitting an enquiry does not place an order. The supplier reviews the selection and checks availability, prices and shipping conditions before making an offer. When the quotation arrives, read it to see which pack sizes have been confirmed and what arrangements are stated for your requested labelling language and delivery date. Do not assume a requested pack size or labelling arrangement is available unless the quotation addresses it. If something in the reply is unclear or differs from your enquiry, ask for clarification before treating any detail as settled.\par {\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "https://bulk-seeds-international.github.io/bulkseedsinternational-en/seed-enquiry-checklist-pack-sizes-labelling-language/"}{\fldrslt Seed enquiry checklist: specify retail pack sizes and labelling language}}\par {\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "https://bulk-seeds-international.github.io/bulkseedsinternational-en/dateien/minimax-3bb22ceba0ff15c3c39ca694/inhalt.odt"}{\fldrslt Related document}}\par }